In this experiment, I learned how different tokenizers divide sentences into smaller parts called tokens. Tokenization is an important step in Natural Language Processing because language models process text in the form of tokens instead of directly understanding complete sentences. For this experiment, I used three different tokenization methods: BPE, WordPiece, and SentencePiece. I used GPT-2 for BPE, Multilingual BERT for WordPiece, and XLM-R for SentencePiece. First, I installed the required packages using requirements.txt. Then I created a Python program named tokenizer_comparison.py and loaded the three pretrained tokenizers using the Transformers library. I prepared sample sentences in English, Telugu, and Hindi to compare how each tokenizer handles different languages. After loading the tokenizers, I passed each sentence to all three tokenizers. The program converted the sentences into token IDs and displayed the corresponding tokens on the screen. It also displayed the total number of tokens generated by each tokenizer. After executing the program, I observed that the same sentence could be divided into different tokens depending on the tokenizer being used. I also understood that different languages may produce different tokenization results because every tokenizer has its own vocabulary and working method. Through this experiment, I learned the basic working of tokenization and understood how text is prepared before it is processed by a language model.
